Spring Boot project structure, explaining how it is organized and how the build process works:

Spring Boot Project Structure Overview
.mvn & .gitignore: Contains Maven Wrapper configurations and files to exclude from version control (e.g., build artifacts like the target folder).
src/main: The heart of the application.
java/: Contains your source code and the main application class.
resources/: Houses configuration files like application.properties and static assets.
src/test: Dedicated directory for unit and integration testing of your application code.
Maven Configuration (pom.xml)
pom.xml (Project Object Model): The core configuration file for dependencies, plugins, and project metadata.
Parent POM: Uses spring-boot-starter-parent to inherit essential dependencies and version management.
Dependencies: Manages project-specific libraries.
Plugins: The spring-boot-maven-plugin is crucial here, as it enables the packaging of the application into a Fat JAR.
The Build Process
target/ Folder: Created after running mvn package. It contains the compiled bytecode and the final executable JAR.
Fat JAR vs. Original JAR:
A Fat JAR is a self-contained executable that includes all dependencies and an embedded server (like Tomcat), 
allowing you to run it with java -jar without external infrastructure.
Repackaging: The Maven plugin performs a "repackage" process where it wraps the original code with all necessary dependencies to create the final,
heavy-duty Fat JAR.
